//
// AUTH MODEL: these routes sit BEFORE the cookie wall (a peer box has no login
// cookie), same as the mail/checkout server-to-server routes. But unlike those,
// the auth here is NOT network position -- this router binds 0.0.0.0, so a
// remoteAddress/header check would only be as safe as trusting Fly's forwarded
// traffic (the same reason channel dispatch moved to a 127.0.0.1 server). The
// mesh auth is the HMAC itself: every message must be signed by a PINNED peer
// secret (mesh-contract verify()), and the read routes require the same
// per-peer HMAC proof. An unsigned/forged request is rejected by the contract,
// independent of where it came from -- which is stronger than an address check.
